---
import Base from '../../layouts/Base.astro';
import { apps, totals } from '../../data/wider-benchmark';

const published = '2026-11-22';
const wall = 'https://github.com/weareaisle/wall-of-fame';
const jsonLd = {
  '@context': 'https://schema.org', '@type': 'TechArticle',
  headline: 'Nine real CVEs, one Mac Studio: where a local scanner finds bugs and where it does not',
  datePublished: published,
  author: { '@type': 'Organization', name: 'Epicworks s.r.o.' }, publisher: { '@type': 'Organization', name: 'Epicworks s.r.o.' },
};
---
<Base
  title="Nine real CVEs, one Mac Studio: where a local scanner finds bugs | EpicScan research"
  description="We scanned nine published CVEs in libpng, GnuTLS, MuPDF, FFmpeg, alsa-lib, ImageMagick, libgit2, rsync and nginx with a local model plus a Claude Opus 4.6 check. Honest results, including the misses."
  path="/research/wider-benchmark-9-cves"
  jsonLd={jsonLd}
>
  <section class="page-hero">
    <div class="container narrow hero-inner" style="gap: 20px;">
      <span class="eyebrow" style="color: var(--teal);">Benchmark report · <time datetime={published}>22 Nov 2026</time></span>
      <h1 class="h1">Nine real CVEs, one Mac Studio</h1>
      <p class="lead">Our first benchmark looked good: two published CVEs, both found. So we made the test harder. Nine more real vulnerabilities, nine different applications, 54 runs. This is what a local scanner finds, what it misses, and what a frontier model as a second reviewer can and cannot fix.</p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section" style="padding-top: 24px;">
    <article class="container prose">
      <h2>The short version</h2>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>The local model found the bug in {totals.foundRuns} of {totals.vulnerableRuns} vulnerable runs</strong>, covering {totals.cvesFound} of the 9 CVEs. It was reliable on a classic stack overflow (GnuTLS, 3 of 3) and missed double frees, edge cases and logic errors.</li>
        <li><strong>No patched file kept a finding about its fixed bug</strong> ({totals.patchedClean} of 27).</li>
        <li><strong>The Opus check works as a filter.</strong> It confirmed every finding about a real CVE and rejected {totals.noiseRejected} of {totals.noise} false or unrelated findings (84%).</li>
        <li><strong>It cannot fix detection.</strong> In a second pass Opus re-checked all {totals.rejectedRechecked} findings the local triage had thrown away. It recovered none of the missed bugs, because none of them described the real bug.</li>
        <li><strong>Cost:</strong> {totals.scanHours} hours of local scanning on one Mac Studio, and USD {totals.opusTotalUsd.toFixed(2)} for both Opus passes over 54 runs.</li>
      </ul>

      <h2>The test</h2>
      <p>We picked nine CVEs from <a href={wall} rel="noopener">AISLE's public list</a> of vulnerabilities they discovered and disclosed, one per application, each with a public fix commit that changes a single file. For each one we copied that file at the vulnerable commit and at the fix commit. Two candidates were dropped: curl CVE-2025-10966 (the fix deleted the file, so there is no patched version) and wolfSSH CVE-2025-11624 (the file is larger than the scanner's limit).</p>
      <p>Setup as in our <a href="/research/local-qwen-opus-validation">first benchmark</a>: nano-analyzer with Qwen 3.6 35B A3B running locally on our Mac Studio, 5 triage rounds, three runs per file. Claude Opus 4.6 then checked every finding the local triage kept, without being told which bug to expect. We used the Opus check only on public open-source code; for customers it is an optional paid extra, because it sends the flagged file to Anthropic.</p>

      <h2>Results by application</h2>
      <div class="scroll-x">
        <table class="table">
          <thead><tr><th scope="col">Application</th><th scope="col">CVE</th><th scope="col">Bug class</th><th scope="col">Found</th><th scope="col">Patched clean</th></tr></thead>
          <tbody>
            {apps.map((a) => (
              <tr>
                <td><a href={`/research/${a.slug}`}>{a.app}</a></td>
                <td>{a.cve}</td>
                <td>{a.bugClass}</td>
                <td><strong>{a.vulnerable.filter((r) => r.found).length}/3</strong></td>
                <td>3/3</td>
              </tr>
            ))}
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>
      <p>Each application has its own report with the bug, the run-by-run numbers and what the models said.</p>

      <h2>What we learned</h2>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>Overflows with an obvious length mismatch are found.</strong> GnuTLS copies strlen(label) bytes into a 32-byte buffer; every run found it.</li>
        <li><strong>Lifetime and logic bugs are mostly missed.</strong> A wrong pointer freed (ImageMagick) was found once in three runs. Double frees in exception paths (MuPDF, alsa-lib), an inverted comparison (libgit2), an off-by-one in a read loop (rsync) and an empty-input edge case (nginx) were never found.</li>
        <li><strong>Near is not found.</strong> In nginx and rsync the local model looked at the right function, even the right data structure, but described a different, already-guarded problem. Opus correctly rejected those claims.</li>
        <li><strong>The second reviewer is a filter, not a finder.</strong> Opus removed 84% of the noise and kept every real finding, but it can only judge what the scanner describes. More detection needs a stronger scanning model or more context, not only a stronger reviewer.</li>
        <li><strong>One verdict is not proof.</strong> On a few unrelated claims Opus said "confirmed" in one run and "false positive" in another. Repeated runs show where the checker itself is unsure. Opus confirmed 8 unrelated findings in total; we have not verified them and do not describe them until we have.</li>
      </ul>

      <h2>Compared with our first benchmark</h2>
      <p>On the two cases AISLE published with its scanner (FreeBSD CVE-2026-4747 and OpenSSL CVE-2025-11187), our local setup matched GPT-OSS-120B in AISLE's table. These nine CVEs were never part of a published scanner benchmark, and most were found by AISLE's full research system rather than the open-source scanner alone. That makes this a much harder and more honest test, and the results show it.</p>

      <h2>Limits</h2>
      <ul>
        <li>Three runs per file and one file per CVE. The scanner sees one file only, not the whole project.</li>
        <li>The models may have seen some of these fixes during training. For the misses, that clearly did not help.</li>
        <li>Opus prices are list prices at the time of the test; local scan time is wall-clock time on one Mac Studio.</li>
      </ul>

      <h2>What we change next</h2>
      <p>The bottleneck is detection, so the next step is testing a stronger model for the scan itself on the missed cases, measured the same way, with the cost per scan. We will publish that result too, whatever it is.</p>
      <p class="muted">Questions or raw run data: <a href="mailto:scan@epicworks.cz">scan@epicworks.cz</a>.</p>
      <p><a class="btn btn-primary" href="/early-access">Request early access</a></p>
    </article>
  </section>
</Base>
